  (*) with the exception that any executable packer may be used.

vmdos's changes (marked "vmdos:" in the source):
- MORE: read the input with DOS's read call. MORE reads piped or redirected
  input from a duplicate of STDIN, a handle the C library didn't open, and
  Open Watcom's read() refuses those: `TYPE X | MORE` and `MORE < X` showed
  nothing.
- DELTREE: the "Do you REALLY want to do this??" prompt (DELTREE /Y in a
  root folder) lacked the $ that ends a DOS string, so it printed memory
  after it.
- MOVE: built with a 16 KB stack (build.sh): the 2 KB Watcom default gave
  "Stack Overflow!".
- ATTRIB: built with Open Watcom instead of Borland C (no source change).
- LESS: src/ leaves out the test suite (lesstest/) and the Unicode data
  (unicode/), and has help.c, funcs.h and lessmsg.inc generated already
  (make -f Makefile.aut help.c funcs.h lessmsg.inc).

ZIP and UNZIP: from https://github.com/kevin1024/infozip-dos16-builds,
commit 0261a3f, built here with its build.sh (Open Watcom; the C sources
unmodified, its Watcom makefiles adapted to a POSIX host), the same bytes as
its release. Not in src/ (12 MB); the Info-ZIP license (src/ of that repo)
asks for none. They warn that TZ isn't set (no UTC times): SET TZ=EST5EDT
(or yours) in AUTOEXEC.BAT stops that.

Not here: EDIT, TREE and SUBST (vmdos's own: dos/edit, dos/tree,
dos/subst.asm), WHICH (FreeCOM has it built in), JOIN (FreeDOS's kernel
doesn't handle joined drives: see dos/subst.asm), KEYB (FreeDOS's FD-KEYB is
Turbo Pascal; mKEYB does the same with the layouts built in: MKEYB GR).
FreeDOS EDIT (0.9a/0.9c) was here first, and left: in vmdos it stopped
with an invalid opcode on about half its starts (a window's class read back
as garbage, then a call through a null pointer); not found why.
